# 2015--2018: every question inspected, including exclusions.
omit(2015,1,1,3,'Comparing the five powers needs repeated small power comparisons but no substantial new idea.')
omit(2015,1,2,3.5,'Factoring b squared minus a squared forces the two factors to be 1 and p squared; both conclusions then follow directly.')
add(2015,1,3,4.5,'Combinatorics',r'A corridor has ten rooms on each side. In how many ways can seven rooms be reserved if no two reserved rooms on the same side are adjacent?','One must split the choice between the two sides and count nonadjacent selections using a gap transformation.',r'The number of ways to choose $k$ nonadjacent positions from ten is $\binom{11-k}{k}$: subtract $0,1,\ldots,k-1$ from the increasing chosen positions. Each side holds at most five reservations, so the answer is $\sum_{k=2}^{5}\binom{11-k}{k}\binom{4+k}{7-k}=2(36\cdot6+56\cdot35)=4352$.',['Counting','Nonadjacent selections'])
add(2015,1,4,4.5,'Algebra',r'For a real number $x$, suppose $t=x+x^{-1}$ is an integer greater than $2$. Prove $t_n=x^n+x^{-n}$ is an integer for every positive integer $n$, and determine exactly when $t\mid t_n$.','The useful recurrence is not stated; reducing it modulo t then separates odd and even indices.',r'Set $t_0=2$ and $t_1=t$. Expanding gives $t_{n+1}=tt_n-t_{n-1}$, so all terms are integers. Modulo $t$ this becomes $t_{n+1}\equiv-t_{n-1}$. Thus odd-indexed terms are $0$ modulo $t$, whereas $t_{2j}\equiv2(-1)^j$. Since $t>2$, exactly the odd positive $n$ work.',['Linear recurrences','Divisibility'])
add(2015,1,5,6,'Geometry',r'In cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$, let $F$ be the midpoint of the arc $AB$ avoiding $C,D$. Put $P=DF\cap AC$ and $Q=CF\cap BD$. Prove $PQ\parallel AB$.','Equal arc lengths must be combined with intersecting diagonal ratios; the required parallel line is not a direct angle chase.',None,['Cyclic quadrilaterals','Similarity'])
add(2015,1,6,7,'Algebra',r'Find all functions $f:\mathbb Z_{>0}\to\mathbb Z_{>0}$ such that $1/a+1/b=1/c$ always implies $1/f(a)+1/f(b)=1/f(c)$.','The condition is restricted to integer Egyptian-fraction identities, so recovering all values from a single scale requires a carefully chosen induction.',None,['Functional equations','Egyptian fractions'])
add(2015,2,1,5,'Algebra',r'Let $x_1=2014$ and $x_{n+1}=((\sqrt2+1)x_n-1)/(\sqrt2+1+x_n)$. Find $x_{2015}$.','Recognising a tangent subtraction or a projective transformation reveals a short period hidden by the irrational coefficients.',r'Write $x_1=\tan\theta$. Since $\tan(\pi/8)=\sqrt2-1$, the recurrence gives $x_n=\tan(\theta-(n-1)\pi/8)$, with period eight. None of these terms is undefined: a pole would force $2014$ to be one of the tangent values at multiples of $\pi/8$. Since $2014\equiv6\pmod8$, $x_{2015}=\tan(\theta+\pi/4)=(2014+1)/(1-2014)=-2015/2013$.',['Periodic sequences','Trigonometry'])
add(2015,2,2,5,'Combinatorics',r'A school has an odd number of classes, each with an odd number of pupils. A council selects one pupil per class. Prove that there are more councils with an odd number of boys than with an odd number of girls if and only if an odd number of classes have more boys than girls.','A signed count packages all councils into one product; interpreting its sign uses both oddness assumptions.',r'In class $i$, let $b_i,g_i$ be the numbers of boys and girls. Expanding $\prod_i(g_i-b_i)$ counts councils with an even number of boys positively and those with an odd number negatively. Since the council size is odd, even-boy councils are exactly odd-girl councils. Each $g_i-b_i$ is nonzero because its class size is odd. The product is negative exactly when an odd number of factors are negative, which is exactly the claimed equivalence.',['Generating functions','Parity','Signed counting'])
add(2015,2,3,7,'Geometry',r'Two circles touch internally at $A$. A chord $PQ$ of the outer circle is tangent to the inner circle. Prove that the incentre of $APQ$ traces a third circle tangent to both given circles at $A$.','The angle-bisector geometry must be converted into a fixed relation for the incentre, and a locus proof must establish both directions.',None,['Loci','Incentre','Tangency'])
omit(2015,2,4,7.5,'Constructing a 100-cycle in the integer visibility graph while excluding every non-neighbour requires a substantial congruence construction.')
add(2016,1,1,4,'Number theory',r'Anna buys one book each day from 1 January 2015 through 31 December 2016 inclusive. She buys a shelf on the first day and every fourteen days thereafter. On how many of these days can she distribute all books equally among all shelves?','Converting dates to intervals with a fixed shelf count gives a divisibility count, with a separate final partial interval.',None,['Divisibility','Floor functions'])
add(2016,1,2,4.5,'Geometry',r'Let $ABCD$ be cyclic, and let $E=CD\cap BA$. The tangent at $D$ to circle $ADE$ meets line $CB$ at $F$. Prove triangle $CDF$ is isosceles.','A tangent-chord equality on the auxiliary circle has to be transferred through the original cyclic quadrilateral.',None,['Tangent-chord theorem','Cyclic quadrilaterals'])
add(2016,1,3,5,'Algebra',r'Let $t_n=An^2+Bn+C$ for $n\ge0$, with real $A\ne0,B,C$. Determine coefficients giving the longest possible block of successive terms which is also a block of successive Fibonacci numbers, where $F_0=0,F_1=1$.','Finite differences restrict which Fibonacci blocks can agree with a quadratic; the upper bound must be matched by coefficients.',r'For four successive Fibonacci terms beginning $u,v$, the third difference is $2v-3u$. Four can therefore agree with a quadratic only when $2v=3u$, which occurs for the consecutive Fibonacci values $u=2,v=3$. These give $2,3,5,8$, whose first differences are $1,2,3$. Thus $t_n=(n^2+n)/2+2$ matches four terms starting at $n=0$. Five terms cannot match because the next third difference would also need to vanish, requiring $2(u+v)=3v$ alongside $2v=3u$, impossible for these positive values. Hence four is the maximum.',['Finite differences','Fibonacci'])
add(2016,1,4,6,'Combinatorics',r'Two initially empty jars and a supply of 100 pebbles are given. Each move transfers one pebble from the supply to a jar or from a jar to the supply. The red jar must always contain at least as many pebbles as the blue jar, and no pair of jar counts may repeat. Find the maximum possible number of moves.','The admissible states form a triangular grid; a bipartite-colour bound must be attained by a long explicit path.',None,['Graph paths','Colouring','Extremal constructions'])
add(2016,1,5,6.5,'Geometry',r'Let $D,E,F$ be the perpendicular feet from $A,B,C$ to the opposite side lines of triangle $ABC$. Let $P,Q,R,S$ be the perpendicular feet from $D$ to $BA,BE,CF,CA$. Prove the four points $P,Q,R,S$ are collinear.','Several projection circles have to be connected to obtain a common Simson-type line; one isolated cyclic observation does not suffice.',None,['Simson line','Projections','Cyclic quadrilaterals'])
add(2016,1,6,6.5,'Number theory',r'Call an integer charming if it is $2$ or $3^i5^j$ for nonnegative integers $i,j$. Prove that every positive integer is a sum of distinct charming integers.','A naive ternary induction fails at the exceptional summand 2; a stronger representation invariant or careful simultaneous induction is needed.',None,['Induction','Representations','Powers'])
add(2016,2,1,6,'Geometry',r'Three circles of radii $r_1,r_2,r_3$ are pairwise externally tangent and share a tangent touching them at $A,B,C$, with $B$ between $A,C$. Prove $16(r_1+r_2+r_3)\ge9(AB+BC+CA)$.','The common tangent gives square-root radius relations; the sharp constant emerges only after using the middle-circle constraint.',None,['Tangency','Inequalities'])
omit(2016,2,2,7.5,'Finding the optimal information guarantee needs both an adversarial partial-order construction and a matching strategy.')
add(2016,2,3,7,'Geometry',r'In cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$, write $P=AC\cap BD$ and $Q=DA\cap CB$, and let $E$ be the midpoint of $AB$. Prove that $PQ\perp AC$ implies $PE\perp BC$.','A relation between a diagonal intersection and an external intersection must be translated into a midpoint orthogonality; coordinates or pole-polar ideas require sustained work.',None,['Cyclic quadrilaterals','Perpendicularity','Midpoints'])
add(2016,2,4,6.5,'Number theory',r'Let $p$ be prime and let distinct positive integers $u,v$ satisfy $u^2+v^2=2p^2$. Prove that $2p-u-v$ is a square or twice a square.','The identity involving the sum and difference gives a square product, but a careful gcd and parity analysis is needed to isolate each factor.',None,['Sum of squares','GCD','Diophantine equations'])
omit(2017,1,1,3,'Count odd digits separately at each place value, with a short correction for the final partial block.')
omit(2017,1,2,3.5,'The two breakpoints divide the equation into three elementary power equations.')
omit(2017,1,3,3,'Completing squares gives a difference of squares equal to minus five and an immediate factor-pair check.')
add(2017,1,4,5.5,'Combinatorics',r'Naomi and Tom alternately select unused integers from $1$ to $100$, Naomi first. A player loses if the sum of every integer selected so far cannot be expressed as a difference of two integer squares. Determine who has a winning strategy.','The arithmetic condition becomes a residue restriction; one must then control an adversary using the inventory of residue classes.',None,['Games','Modular arithmetic','Pairing strategies'])
add(2017,1,5,6.5,'Geometry',r'In triangle $ABC$, suppose $\angle A<\angle B<90^\circ$. Tangents at $A,C$ to its circumcircle meet at $P$, and extensions of $AB,PC$ meet at $Q$. If $[ACP]=[ABC]=[BQC]$, prove $\angle BCA=90^\circ$.','The equal areas encode two side ratios; combining them with the tangent geometry to force a right angle is the substantive step.',None,['Area','Tangents','Similarity'])
add(2017,1,6,5.5,'Number theory',r'For an odd positive integer $n$, find the least positive $m$ for which $n,n+2,n+4,n+6$ divide $m,m+1,m+2,m+3$, respectively.','Multiplying the congruences by two reveals a common multiple; positivity and parity then identify its least allowable value.',r'All four conditions are equivalent to the respective odd moduli dividing $2m-n$. Put $L=\operatorname{lcm}(n,n+2,n+4,n+6)$. Then $2m-n$ is an odd multiple of $L$, because both $n$ and $L$ are odd. A negative multiple would give $m\le(n-L)/2<0$, while the smallest positive multiple gives $m=(n+L)/2$. This is an integer and satisfies all four conditions.',['Congruences','Least common multiple'])
add(2017,2,1,5.5,'Number theory',r'For how many positive integers $n<2017$ is there an isosceles right triangle with integer-coordinate vertices having exactly $n$ integer-coordinate points on its boundary?','The lattice boundary count reduces to three gcds, whose parity structure classifies all possible totals.',r'Translate the right-angle vertex to the origin. The legs are $(a,b)$ and $(-b,a)$, up to orientation. The boundary count is $2\gcd(a,b)+\gcd(a+b,a-b)$. Write $a=gu,b=gv$ with coprime $u,v$. The last gcd is $g$ if $u,v$ have different parity, and $2g$ if both are odd. Thus the count is $3g$ or $4g$. Both occur for every $g>0$, using $(u,v)=(1,0)$ or $(1,1)$. The answer is $\lfloor2016/3\rfloor+\lfloor2016/4\rfloor-\lfloor2016/12\rfloor=1008$.',['Lattice geometry','GCD','Counting'])
add(2017,2,2,6,'Number theory',r'Let $a_n=\frac1n\sum_{k=1}^n\lfloor n/k\rfloor$. Prove that $a_{n+1}>a_n$ infinitely often, and determine whether $a_{n+1}<a_n$ infinitely often.','The increasing subsequences follow from harmonic growth, while decreases require recognising divisor counts at prime indices.',r'Because $\lfloor n/k\rfloor>n/k-1$, we have $a_n>\sum_{k=1}^n1/k-1$, so $a_n$ is unbounded and must increase infinitely often. Write $D(n)=\sum_{k=1}^n\lfloor n/k\rfloor$. The difference $D(n+1)-D(n)$ is the number of positive divisors of $n+1$. When $n+1=p$ is prime it is $2$, so $a_p<a_{p-1}$ exactly when $D(p-1)>2(p-1)$. The harmonic bound ensures this for every sufficiently large prime. Hence decreases also occur infinitely often.',['Divisor sums','Floor functions','Primes'])
add(2017,2,3,7,'Geometry',r'In cyclic quadrilateral $ABCD$, diagonals meet at $P$ and rays $AD,BC$ meet at $Q$. The internal bisector of $\angle BQA$ meets $AC$ at $R$, and that of $\angle APD$ meets $AD$ at $S$. Prove $RS\parallel CD$.','Two angle-bisector ratios occur in different triangles; cyclic chord relations must link them into the precise parallelism ratio.',None,['Angle bisectors','Cyclic quadrilaterals'])
omit(2017,2,4,8,'The optimal three-digit query strategy requires a difficult worst-case information lower bound and construction.')
add(2018,1,1,4.5,'Number theory',r'Helen lists the remainders when $365$ is divided by $1,2,\ldots,365$. Phil does the same with $366$ and divisors $1,2,\ldots,366$. Whose remainders have the greater sum, and by how much?','Compare the two remainder lists termwise, isolating exactly the divisors where wraparound occurs.',r'For each $1\le k\le365$, the remainder increases by $1$ unless $k\mid366$, in which case it changes by $1-k$. Phil also has a final zero remainder for $k=366$. Hence Phil minus Helen is $365-\sum_{k\mid366,\,k<366}k=731-\sigma(366)$. As $366=2\cdot3\cdot61$, $\sigma(366)=3\cdot4\cdot62=744$. Helen wins by $13$.',['Divisor sums','Remainders'])
add(2018,1,2,4.5,'Combinatorics',r'Each of six friends swims on exactly 75 of 100 days. Let $n$ be the number of days when at least five swim. Find the greatest and least possible $n$.','Double counting gives both bounds, but balanced attendance constructions are needed to demonstrate attainability.',r'There are $450$ total attendances. Thus $450\le6n+4(100-n)$ and $5n\le450$, giving $25\le n\le90$. To attain $25$, let everyone swim on 25 days; on each remaining day omit a pair, using each of the 15 pairs five times. Every person is omitted on 25 days. To attain $90$, let nobody swim on ten days; on each of the other 90 days omit one person, each person being omitted fifteen times.',['Double counting','Extremal constructions'])
add(2018,1,3,4.5,'Geometry',r'In isosceles triangle $ABC$, $AB=AC$ and $BC$ is the longest side. Choose $N\in BC$ with $BN=AB$, and let $M$ be its perpendicular projection to $AB$. Prove line $MN$ bisects both the area and the perimeter of the triangle.','The projection length has to be related to the base using the isosceles condition, yielding two different bisection statements.',None,['Area','Projections','Isosceles triangles'])
add(2018,1,4,5,'Algebra',r'Let $a_1=1$ and let all $a_n$ be positive real numbers with $a_{n+1}+a_n=(a_{n+1}-a_n)^2$. How many possible values can $a_{2017}$ have?','Although the terms are real, solving the transition shows they remain triangular numbers and become a constrained walk.',r'Write $T_k=k(k+1)/2$. If $a_n=T_k$, solving the quadratic gives $a_{n+1}=T_{k+1}$ or $T_{k-1}$. Positivity excludes $T_0$. Starting at $k=1$, the index therefore makes steps of $\pm1$ on the positive integers. After 2016 steps it can be any odd $k$ from $1$ to $2017$: first take the necessary upward steps, and use repeated excursions $1,2,1$ for the rest before ascending. There are $1009$ such values.',['Recurrences','Triangular numbers','Walks'])
add(2018,1,5,6.5,'Combinatorics',r'A 100-comb is a $2\times100$ rectangle of unit cells with alternate cells along one long side removed; rotations are permitted. What is the least number of cells one must colour in a $200\times200$ grid so that no 100-comb consists entirely of uncoloured cells?','The covering lower bound requires multiple overlapping placements, and attaining it demands a carefully arranged blocking pattern.',None,['Grid coverings','Extremal constructions'])
add(2018,1,6,6,'Combinatorics',r'Cards numbered $1$ to $300$ are selected without replacement into a row. After every selection the mean of the selected numbers must be an integer. Selection stops only when no remaining card can be appended legally. What is the shortest possible final row? Give an example.','Each next card is constrained by a changing modulus; proving a short row can exhaust its entire admissible residue class is the key construction.',None,['Congruences','Constructive counting','Means'])
add(2018,2,1,5,'Geometry',r'In triangle $ABC$, let $M$ be the midpoint of $AC$. A circle tangent to $BC$ at $B$ passes through $M$ and meets line $AB$ again at $P$. Prove $AB\cdot BP=2BM^2$.','A tangent-chord similarity must be paired with the median relation; the factor two comes from the midpoint rather than the circle alone.',None,['Tangency','Midpoints','Similarity'])
add(2018,2,2,7,'Combinatorics',r'Alice sits at one of $n$ circularly arranged places and eats its cake. The Mad Hatter prescribes integers $a_1,\ldots,a_{n-1}$. On move $i$, Alice moves $a_i$ places in either direction of her choice and eats any cake there. For which $n$ can the Hatter force her to eat every cake?','Both a forcing construction and an obstruction to all possible instruction lists are needed; divisibility and signed partial sums interact.',None,['Games','Cyclic groups','Powers of two'])
add(2018,2,3,6.5,'Number theory',r'Does there exist a positive integer $m$ for which $(m+1)^3+(m+2)^3+\cdots+(2m)^3$ is a perfect square?','The sum-of-cubes identity leads to an indefinite quadratic equation; resolving existence requires more than testing small values.',None,['Pell equations','Sums of powers'])
omit(2018,2,4,8,'Monotone functions whose 2018th iterate is integer-valued require subtle order-theoretic constructions and obstructions.')
