# Archive seasons 2023--2026.
omit(2023,1,1,3.5,'Counting leading-two house numbers in three intervals reduces the problem to a few divisibility checks.')
add(2023,1,2,4.5,'Number theory',r'For positive integers $a,b$, define $a_1=a,a_2=b$, $a_{2n+1}=a_{2n}a_{2n-1}$ and $a_{2n+2}=a_{2n+1}+4$. What is the largest possible number of squares among $a_1,\ldots,a_{2022}$?','The paired recurrence traps most odd-indexed terms strictly between squares, while an exceptional start makes nearly every even-indexed term square.',None,['Recurrences','Between squares'])
add(2023,1,3,4.5,'Geometry',r'In an acute non-isosceles triangle $ABC$, let $B_1,C_1$ be the midpoints of $AC,AB$. Choose $D$ on ray $BC$ beyond $C$. Points $F,G$ satisfy $\angle AFC=\angle AGB=90^\circ$, $\angle DCF=\angle FCA$, and $\angle CBG=\angle GBA$. Prove $B_1,C_1,F,G$ are collinear.','Each right triangle converts an angle bisector into a midpoint-circle relation; both constructions must be linked to the same midline.',None,['Midpoints','Angle bisectors','Right triangles'])
add(2023,1,4,5.5,'Combinatorics',r'Alex and Katy alternately mark empty cells of an $8\times8$ board, Alex first. Alex marks one cell with A; Katy marks two edge-adjacent cells with K. Play stops when the next player cannot move. What is the largest number of Ks Katy can guarantee?','An optimal domino-packing strategy needs both a guaranteed placement scheme and an adversarial upper bound.',None,['Games','Dominoes','Strategy'])
add(2023,1,5,6,'Number theory',r'Let $f(n)$ count lists of distinct positive integers starting at $1$ and ending at $n$, with every term dividing its successor. Prove that every positive integer $N$ divides $f(n)$ for some $n$.','Prime powers supply only powers of two; adjoining another prime creates a controllable linear factor in the count.',None,['Ordered factorisations','Divisibility','Counting'])
add(2023,1,6,5.5,'Geometry',r'A unit circle has its centre at distance $d$ from a line $l$, where $0<d<2$. A frog starts at a circle point less than one unit from $l$. It makes unit-length jumps alternately to $l$ and to the circle. Prove that after finitely many jumps it revisits a point.','The apparently continuous motion has a hidden two-level coordinate invariant that restricts it to finitely many points.',r'Choose coordinates with the circle centred at $(0,0)$ and $l$ given by $y=d$. Suppose a circle point $P=(x,y)$ jumps to $L=(u,d)$. Circle points one unit from $L$ lie on the intersection of the two unit circles centred at $0$ and $L$. If the next jump does not return to $P$, the other intersection is $P\prime=L-P=(u-x,d-y)$, by the symmetry of equal intersecting circles. Hence each visit to the original circle has height either the initial height $y_0$ or $d-y_0$. Each horizontal line meets the circle in at most two points, so there are at most four possible circle positions. A circle position must therefore repeat.',['Coordinate geometry','Invariants','Pigeonhole principle'])
add(2023,2,1,6,'Geometry',r'Triangle $ABC$ has obtuse angle $A$ and incentre $I$. Circles $ABI,ACI$ meet $BC$ again at $X,Y$. Let $P=AX\cap BI$ and $Q=AY\cap CI$. Prove $BCQP$ is cyclic.','The two auxiliary circles and internal angle bisectors create a coupled angle configuration; the obtuse case requires attention to point order.',None,['Incentre','Cyclic quadrilaterals'])
add(2023,2,2,7,'Combinatorics',r'Start with the list $1,2,\ldots,n$, where $n>1$. You may cyclically replace three consecutive entries $(x,y,z)$ by $(y,z,x)$ when $3\mid x+y+z$, or swap two consecutive entries when their difference is divisible by $3$. For which $n$ can the list become $n,1,2,\ldots,n-1$?','The permitted local permutations preserve a residue-sensitive order invariant; sufficiency requires constructing the full cyclic shift.',None,['Permutations','Invariants','Modular arithmetic'])
add(2023,2,3,5,'Combinatorics',r'For $n\ge3$, consider lists $(a_1,\ldots,a_n)$ with every $a_i\in\{1,\ldots,n\}$. For each $k<n$, let $M_k$ be the least positive possible absolute difference between the means of the first $k$ and first $k+1$ terms. Count lists attaining $M_k$ simultaneously for every $k$.','Minimum rational mean differences force a very rigid recurrence, but the first two transitions must be treated separately.',r'The difference is $|ka_{k+1}-(a_1+\cdots+a_k)|/[k(k+1)]$. Its least positive value is $1/[k(k+1)]$, attained by taking one of the first $k$ entries to be 2, all the others and the next entry 1. Thus an ideal list has $a_2=a_1\pm1$, and the next condition gives $a_3\in\{a_1,a_2\}$. Write $c=a_3$. Its first three entries have sum $3c\pm1$. For $k\ge3$, if the current sum is $kc\pm1$, the next condition forces $a_{k+1}=c$, since $k(a_{k+1}-c)$ must be $0$ or $\pm2$. This preserves the form. Choose the adjacent pair of values in $n-1$ ways, their order in two ways and $a_3$ in two ways. The answer is $4(n-1)$.',['Means','Recurrences','Counting'])
add(2023,2,4,7,'Number theory',r'A triangle has positive integer side lengths $a,b,c$ with $\gcd(a,b,c)=1$, opposite angles $A,B,C$. If $A=3B$, prove that at least one of $a,b,c$ is a cube.','The triple-angle sine relation gives a Diophantine parametrisation; primitivity must then force a perfect cube among the side factors.',None,['Integer triangles','Trigonometric identities','Coprimality'])
add(2024,1,1,4,'Combinatorics',r'A typist permutes the distinct letters of a word, with every letter appearing exactly once and never more than one position later than intended. How many outputs are possible for OLYMPIADS? Prove the count.','A useful recurrence comes from the last position rather than tracking how far letters move early.',r'For a word of $n$ distinct labelled letters, only letters $n-1$ and $n$ can occupy the last position. If letter $n$ is last, the first $n-1$ letters form an admissible smaller instance. If letter $n-1$ is last, relabel the remaining letter $n$ as $n-1$; again the first $n-1$ positions form an admissible instance, since neither has a restrictive upper bound there. These are reversible correspondences, so $A_n=2A_{n-1}$, with $A_1=1$. OLYMPIADS has nine letters, giving $2^8=256$.',['Permutations','Recurrences'])
omit(2024,1,2,2.5,'Successive differences either stay unchanged or multiply by minus two, so an ending difference of one immediately determines the starting magnitude.')
add(2024,1,3,5,'Geometry',r'Let $\angle ACB<\angle BAC<90^\circ$ in triangle $ABC$. Choose $X\in AC$ and $Y$ on its circumcircle, both distinct from $A$, with $BX=BY=BA$. Line $XY$ meets the circumcircle again at $Z$. Prove $BZ\perp AC$.','The equal distances introduce a second circle, and its power relations must be translated into a perpendicular chord direction.',None,['Power of a point','Equal chords','Perpendicularity'])
add(2024,1,4,6,'Number theory',r'Find all positive integers $n$ for which $n2^n+1$ is a perfect square.','Factoring the neighbouring odd square roots gives severe 2-adic constraints; an exponential-versus-linear bound is needed to finish all cases.',None,['Valuations','Diophantine equations','Exponential bounds'])
add(2024,1,5,5,'Combinatorics',r'Colour 1000 equally spaced points around a circle red or blue. Count one fault for every adjacent red pair and one for every blue pair with exactly one point between them. What is the least possible number of faults?','A local four-point obstruction yields a global bound, attained by a periodic colouring.',r'In any four consecutive positions there is either an adjacent red pair or a blue pair at distance two. Indeed, if positions 1 and 3 are not both blue and positions 2 and 4 are not both blue, avoiding adjacent reds is impossible by checking whether position 2 is red or blue. Partition the 1000 positions into 250 consecutive blocks of four. Each block contributes a fault wholly within it, so there are at least 250 faults. Repeating the pattern RRBB creates exactly one adjacent red pair per block and no blue pair at distance two, giving 250.',['Colouring','Local-to-global counting'])
add(2024,1,6,7,'Geometry',r'A convex equilateral $n$-gon, $n>4$, also has all diagonals joining vertices three steps apart equal. For which $n$ must all its interior angles be equal?','Equal three-edge chord lengths constrain neighbouring turning angles; the resulting periodicity must be reconciled with convex closure and counterexamples.',None,['Polygons','Periodicity','Trigonometry'])
add(2024,2,1,5.5,'Number theory',r'Form the decimal integers $7,76,769,7692,76923,769230,\ldots$ by taking successive initial digits of $10/13=0.769230\ldots$. Prove that at least 49 of the first 60 terms have at least three prime factors counted with multiplicity.','The six-digit period permits factorisations by residue class of the length; sufficient prime-factor multiplicities must be established for almost all cases.',None,['Periodic decimals','Factorisation'])
add(2024,2,2,6,'Algebra',r'Find all functions $f:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z$ satisfying $2f(f(n))=5f(n)-2n$ for every integer $n$.','Iteration produces a linear recurrence with one contracting root; integrality rules out that component but must be used rigorously.',None,['Functional equations','Linear recurrences','Integrality'])
add(2024,2,3,6.5,'Geometry',r'In acute triangle $ABC$ with $AB>AC$, tangents at $B,C$ to the circumcircle meet at $P$. The line through the midpoints of $PB,PC$ meets $AB,AC$ at $X,Y$. Prove $AXPY$ is cyclic.','The midline of the tangent triangle controls two intersection ratios; these must combine with a symmedian relation to yield cyclicity.',None,['Tangents','Midpoints','Symmedians'])
add(2024,2,4,6.5,'Combinatorics',r'Let $m<n$ be positive integers. Start with $n$ piles of $m$ objects. Each operation chooses two piles and removes a total of $n$ objects from them. For which $(m,n)$ can all piles be emptied?','A graph of operations yields a component-size divisibility obstruction, which can be matched by a chain construction.',r'Put $g=\gcd(m,n)$. Form a graph whose vertices are piles and whose edges are operations. In a connected component with $v$ vertices and $e$ operations, $mv=ne$, hence $v\ge n/g$. Connectivity gives $e\ge v-1$, so $(n-m)v\le n$ and therefore $n-m\le g$. Since $g\mid n-m$, this forces $n-m=g$. Conversely suppose $n=kg,m=(k-1)g$. Divide the $n$ piles into $g$ groups of $k$ piles. Within each group, empty the first pile by taking its $(k-1)g$ objects and $g$ from the next. Then empty the remaining $(k-2)g$ of the second with $2g$ from the third, and continue. The final operation removes $g$ from the penultimate and $(k-1)g$ from the last. This empties each group. Thus exactly $n-m=\gcd(m,n)$ works.',['Graph components','Divisibility','Constructions'])
add(2025,1,1,4.5,'Number theory',r'Call $n\ge3$ happy if $n$ distinct positive integers can be arranged in a circle so that two numbers are related by divisibility exactly when they are neighbours. Determine the happy $n$ with $3\le n\le12$.','Transitivity forces alternating divisibility directions outside the triangle case; even cycles then need explicit prime-based constructions.',None,['Divisibility','Graphs','Constructions'])
add(2025,1,2,4.5,'Combinatorics',r'A magician orders cards $1,\ldots,n$. Starting from an arbitrary integer on a board, each card replaces the board number $x$ by the card number minus $x$. For which $n$ can the order guarantee that the final board number is the negative of the initial one?','Iterating the affine operation leaves an alternating sum; parity obstructions and an equal-sum partition construction determine the answer.',None,['Alternating sums','Parity','Constructions'])
omit(2025,1,3,3,'Induction shows precisely the odd positions are losing: an odd product has even factor difference, while any even number can move to the preceding odd number.')
add(2025,1,4,6.5,'Geometry',r'In acute triangle $ABC$, let $AB<AC<BC$ and let $M$ be the midpoint of $BC$. A point $P$ on segment $AM$ satisfies $CP=AB$ and $\angle PAB=\angle BCP$. Prove $\angle CPB=90^\circ$.','The matching side and angle suggest a similarity, but the median condition is needed to force its scale and the final perpendicularity.',None,['Similarity','Medians','Perpendicularity'])
add(2025,1,5,5.5,'Number theory',r'Let $p$ be prime and let $n>1$ be the least positive integer with $p\mid n^6-1$. Prove $p$ divides at least one of $(n+1)^6-1$ and $(n+2)^6-1$.','Factoring sixth roots into quadratic factors exposes neighbouring roots; the minimality condition removes the wrong direction of shift.',None,['Modular arithmetic','Polynomial roots','Primes'])
add(2025,1,6,7,'Combinatorics',r'A $4\times4\times4$ cube is assembled from 64 unit cubes in three flavours. Prove there are twelve unit cubes of one flavour that can be split into six disjoint pairs, all six centre-to-centre distances equal.','The required pairs must be disjoint as well as congruent; a structured pairing of the spatial grid and a sharpened pigeonhole count are needed.',None,['Pigeonhole principle','Matchings','Spatial grids'])
add(2025,2,1,4,'Number theory',r'Prove that for every positive integer $n$, the number $1/n$ is a finite sum of reciprocals of distinct triangular numbers, where $T_k=k(k+1)/2$.','The triangular denominator suggests a telescoping difference; selecting the finite endpoint is the key observation.',r'Since $1/T_k=2(1/k-1/(k+1))$, we have $\sum_{k=n}^{2n-1}1/T_k=2(1/n-1/(2n))=1/n$. The triangular numbers in this sum are distinct.',['Egyptian fractions','Telescoping sums'])
add(2025,2,2,7,'Geometry',r'In acute triangle $ABC$ with $AB<AC$, let $I$ be the incentre. The perpendicular bisector of $BC$ meets $BI,CI$ at $P,Q$. Circles $BIQ,CIP$ meet again at $X$, and $AX$ meets $BC$ at $D$. Prove $D$ lies on circle $AQP$.','The perpendicular-bisector symmetry links two auxiliary circles, but carrying their intersection relation to the final circle requires several non-obvious steps.',None,['Incentre','Radical axes','Cyclic quadrilaterals'])
add(2025,2,3,5.5,'Combinatorics',r'An $n\times n$ checkerboard is scrambled by swapping entire rows or entire columns. What is the greatest, over all scramblings, of the minimum number of such swaps needed to restore the original colouring?','Only row and column parity types matter, but even boards have a complementary restoration option absent on odd boards.',None,['Permutations','Parity','Optimisation'])
add(2025,2,4,6.5,'Number theory',r'How many positive-integer sequences satisfy $u_1=1$ and $u_{n+1}=(u_n^2+u_n+1)^{2025}/u_{n-1}$ for all $n\ge2$?','The first nontrivial divisibility restricts the second term, and reciprocal-polynomial symmetry propagates integrality indefinitely.',None,['Recurrences','Reciprocal polynomials','Divisibility'])
add(2026,1,1,4,'Number theory',r'A ramp is a triple of distinct positive integers $(a,b,c)$ with $a\mid b$ and $b\mid c$. For each prime $p$ and positive integer $n$, determine whether $p^n$ can be the sum of a ramp.','Factoring out the smallest term reduces the question to a factorisation of a prime power minus one, with a few small-prime exceptions.',None,['Prime powers','Divisibility','Constructions'])
add(2026,1,2,4,'Algebra',r'Find every real triple $(x,y,z)$ satisfying $x^2+2yz=4$, $y^2+2zx=4$ and $z^2+2xy=1$.','Subtracting equations creates two algebraic branches; their real solutions must be checked rather than assuming symmetry.',None,['Polynomial systems','Factorisation'])
add(2026,1,3,4.5,'Combinatorics',r'An $11\times11$ grid is peeled into L-shaped pieces of sizes $21,19,\ldots,3$ and one unit square, by repeatedly removing the current top row and left column. Reassemble the grid using rotations but no reflections, with the unit square in row 3, column 3. How many reassemblies are there?','The longest L must occupy a corner, giving a recursive description; the final square location translates into two independent binomial choices.',r'The largest L has two perpendicular arms spanning the full side of the board, so its corner must be one of the four board corners. Removing it leaves a smaller square; the same argument repeats. At each of the ten stages, independently choose whether to remove the top or bottom row and the left or right column. To leave the final square in original row 3 and column 3, exactly two removed rows must be above it and exactly two removed columns left of it. There are $\binom{10}{2}^2=2025$ choices. Each gives a unique valid nested placement.',['Recursive counting','Binomial coefficients','Tilings'])
add(2026,1,4,6,'Geometry',r'In acute triangle $ABC$ with $AB>AC$, let $M$ be the midpoint of $BC$. The circle through $M$ tangent to $AB$ at $B$ and the circle through $M$ tangent to $AC$ at $C$ meet again at $D$. Prove $MA\cdot MD=MB\cdot MC$.','The two tangent circles provide an inversion-like relation centred at the midpoint; finding the correct angle and ratio chain is the key step.',None,['Tangency','Inversion','Midpoints'])
add(2026,1,5,7,'Number theory',r'Start with $t_1=1$. If $t_n$ is even, put $t_{n+1}=t_n/2$. If $t_n>1$ is odd, divide it by $3$ and round to the nearest integer. If $t_n=1$, put $t_{n+1}=2025k$, where $k$ counts the occurrences of $1$ up to that point. Does the sequence contain every positive integer?','Every excursion terminates, but proving or disproving universal coverage requires analysing inverse branches and their residue classes modulo 2025.',None,['Recurrences','Reverse constructions','Congruences'])
add(2026,1,6,7,'Combinatorics',r'There are 1000 cyclically labelled lily pads, initially with frogs on pads $1,\ldots,n$. A frog may move four places in either direction to an empty pad, or jump three places to an empty pad when both intervening pads are occupied, removing those two frogs. For which $1\le n\le1000$ can exactly one frog remain?','Residue-class parity gives a stronger obstruction than the total frog parity; admissible starts then require a systematic reduction construction.',None,['Solitaire','Invariants','Modular arithmetic'])
add(2026,2,1,5.5,'Number theory',r'For a prime $p>3$, let $k$ be the number of ordered positive-integer pairs $(m,n)$ with $\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)+\gcd(m,n)=p^4$. Find the smallest possible $k$ as $p$ varies.','Factoring out the gcd turns the count into coprime factorisations of four nearby prime powers; lower bounds on distinct prime factors must be simultaneously attained.',None,['GCD and LCM','Prime factors','Counting'])
add(2026,2,2,6.5,'Geometry',r'In convex quadrilateral $ABCD$, suppose $AD$ and $BC$ are not parallel. Let $X=AC\cap BD$, and let $Y$ be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of $AD,BC$, lying strictly inside triangle $XCD$. Prove $AC=BD$ if and only if $XY$ bisects $\angle DXC$.','Equal-distance conditions from the perpendicular bisectors must be combined with the interior angle-bisector condition in both directions.',None,['Perpendicular bisectors','Angle bisectors','Equivalence'])
add(2026,2,3,6,'Combinatorics',r'A $30\times30$ grid contains 300 entries each of $-1,0,1$. Can its thirty row sums and thirty column sums all be different?','Distinct sums almost exhaust their allowed range; a global sum-of-squares inequality then uses the fixed number of nonzero entries.',r'If all 60 sums are different, they occupy 60 of the 61 integers from $-30$ to $30$. Their total is zero, so the omitted value is zero. Their squared sum would be $2\sum_{j=1}^{30}j^2=18910$. Let entries be $a_{ij}$ and row and column sums $r_i,c_j$. Since their totals vanish, expanding $\sum_{i,j}(a_{ij}-r_i/30-c_j/30)^2\ge0$ gives $\sum_i r_i^2+\sum_jc_j^2\le30\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}^2=18000$. This contradiction shows it is impossible.',['Cauchy-Schwarz','Sum of squares','Grids'])
omit(2026,2,4,7.5,'The arbitrary bounded-memory recurrence requires a nontrivial sign-variation or extremal argument to rule out infinitely many positive and negative terms.')
